<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anuradhapura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anuradhapura is a city in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> that was the island's first great capital, from about the 4th century BCE until the 11th century CE. The chronicles credit its founding to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandukabhaya">Pandukabhaya</a>, and it grew into a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> with vast stupas such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruwanwelisaya">Ruwanwelisaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavanaramaya">Jetavanaramaya</a> and the sacred Bodhi tree. It was abandoned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> invasions, and its ruins are a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anuradhapura.jpg" alt="Anuradhapura" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thisaru Tharuka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruwanweli_Maha_Saaya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
